1. Set up GitHub (source code storage)
1.1. Create a GitHub account (if you don't have one) at github.com
1.2. Create a new repository, name it "isdayou"
1.3. Set it to Private
1.4. Add your teammates as collaborators (Settings → Collaborators → add by username or email)
1.5. Clone the repo to your computer (git clone [link])

2. Install your coding tools
2.1. Download and install Git (git-scm.com) — lets your computer talk to GitHub
2.2. Download and install Python 3.11+ (python.org) — for the backend and AI
2.3. Download and install Node.js LTS (nodejs.org) — for the website/frontend
2.4. Download and install Docker Desktop (docker.com) — runs your database without installing it directly
2.5. Download and install VS Code (code.visualstudio.com) — the program you'll write code in
2.6. In VS Code, install the "Python" extension and the "ESLint" extension (search in the Extensions tab)

3. Find and prepare your fish dataset
3.1. Decide how many fish species you want to start with (recommend 10)
3.2. Search for existing labeled fish image datasets (Kaggle.com, search "fish species Philippines" or "fish classification dataset")
3.3. Check BFAR (Bureau of Fisheries and Aquatic Resources) website for any official fish species references or photos you can use
3.4. If you can't find enough images online, plan to take your own photos of each species (ask local markets/fisherfolk for permission)
3.5. Organize the images into folders, one folder per species (e.g., /tilapia/, /bangus/, /galunggong/)
3.6. Make a simple spreadsheet (CSV) listing each species: common name, scientific name, local name, whether it's restricted/has size limits

4. Set up your AI tool (YOLOv11)
4.1. Create a free account on Ultralytics (ultralytics.com) if their tools require it, or just install their Python package — no account needed to use it locally
4.2. Install it by typing in your terminal: pip install ultralytics
4.3. Follow their quickstart guide to confirm it works using their sample dataset first (before using your own fish photos)
4.4. Once confirmed working, replace the sample data with your organized fish photo folders from step 3

5. Set up your database
5.1. In your project folder, create a file called docker-compose.yml
5.2. In it, set up a PostgreSQL database with the PostGIS extension (this handles location/map data later)
5.3. Run docker compose up -d in your terminal to start the database
5.4. Confirm it's running by connecting with a tool like DBeaver (free, download at dbeaver.io)

6. Set up the backend (the "brain" that runs behind the app)
6.1. Inside your repo, create a folder called backend
6.2. Open a terminal in that folder, run python -m venv venv to create a Python workspace
6.3. Activate it, then run pip install fastapi uvicorn sqlalchemy psycopg2-binary
6.4. Create a file main.py with a simple "Hello World" API
6.5. Run uvicorn main:app --reload and check it works by opening localhost:8000 in your browser

7. Set up the frontend (the part users see and tap)
7.1. Inside your repo, create a folder called frontend
7.2. Open a terminal in that folder, run npm create vite@latest . -- --template react
7.3. Run npm install, then npm run dev to confirm the starter page loads
7.4. This is where your login screen, camera button, and fish library page will eventually go

8. Save your progress
8.1. In the main repo folder, run git add .
8.2. Run git commit -m "Initial project setup"
8.3. Run git push
8.4. Confirm your teammates can see the update on GitHub

9. Start building features (once steps 1-8 are done)
9.1. Build login/register screens
9.2. Build the offline fish library (list of fish + info, works without internet)
9.3. Train your YOLOv11 model using your dataset, then connect it to the "scan fish" button
9.4. Build the online-only features: map, location, restricted area alerts

10. Test everything thoroughly
10.1. Test the AI scan feature with fish photos it hasn't seen before (not just your training photos) — check how often it gets the species right
10.2. Test the app fully offline (turn on airplane mode) — confirm scanning, the fish library, and saved history all still work
10.3. Test the online features — confirm GPS, restricted area alerts, and map all load correctly with internet
10.4. Test what happens when you go from offline to online and back — make sure nothing breaks or gets lost
10.5. Have each team member test on their own phone/browser, not just one person's setup
10.6. Write down every bug you find in a simple list (or use GitHub's "Issues" tab to track them)

11. Fix bugs and improve based on testing
11.1. Go through your bug list from step 10.6 and fix them one by one
11.2. If the AI often confuses two species, add more training photos for those two and retrain
11.3. If the app feels slow or clunky, clean up the design/flow before moving on

12. Test with real users (usability testing)
12.1. Ask a few people outside your team (classmates, family, even actual fishers if possible) to try the app
12.2. Watch them use it without helping — see where they get confused
12.3. Ask them simple questions after: was it easy to scan a fish? did they understand the alerts? anything unclear?
12.4. Note down their feedback and fix anything that came up repeatedly

13. Write your documentation
13.1. Write a short technical document: what tools you used, how the system works, how to install/run it
13.2. Write a simple user guide: how a fisher would actually use the app, step by step
13.3. Take screenshots of the app for your documentation and final presentation

14. Prepare your final presentation
14.1. Update your slides with what was actually built (vs. what was originally planned)
14.2. Include screenshots or a short screen recording of the app working, both offline and online
14.3. Prepare to explain your AI results — how accurate is it, on how many species
14.4. Practice explaining the offline/online split clearly, since that's your project's core selling point

15. Final submission
15.1. Make sure your GitHub repo is clean and organized (remove test files, add a README)
15.2. Confirm everything is pushed and up to date
15.3. Submit according to whatever your school/instructor requires (repo link, documentation, presentation, etc.)
